Dear Patrick,
  I posted a solution to your problem a couple days ago, but it had a mistake in it.  I didn't check it carefully.  Here, I think, is a correct solution with the steps broken down for clarity.  Again, I don't know if it's faster.  I hope so.
                   Yours,
                         Ken Straus

z
 [1] 150 150  99  99 150  99  99  99  99 150 150  99  99  99  99 100

> y <- which(z<100)
> y
 [1]  3  4  6  7  8  9 12 13 14 15

>   y[4:10]
[1]  7  8  9 12 13 14 15

>    y[1:7]
[1]  3  4  6  7  8  9 12

>   y[4:10] - y[1:7]

[1] 4 4 3 5 5 5 3

>   y[4:10] - y[1:7] == 3

[1] FALSE FALSE  TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE  TRUE

>  y[4:10][ y[4:10] - y[1:7] == 3 ]
[1]  9 15

>  min(y[4:10][ y[4:10] - y[1:7] == 3 ])
[1] 9

******************************************************************************

> find4th
function(z){
y<-which(z<100)
 return(min(y[4:10][ y[4:10] - y[1:7] == 3 ]))
}

******************************************************************************
> find4th_2
function(z,d){
y<-which(z<d)
n<-length(y)
 return(min(y[4:n][ y[4:n] - y[1:(n-3)] == 3 ]))
}

******************************************************************************

